From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 15:39:19 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: --parallel and #301's stop vote on the AFD
 attention node (world 1)

The glue that runs MiaAI-Lab's GLM multi-stream decode (ashhart/TensorFold#243, its sealed rank messages included)
and stop vote (ashhart/TensorFold#301), the commits before this one, on the split's attention node: one rank (world
1, afd.Solo as its comm), eager batched verify windows over the MCDMA wire, prompts filling between rounds, DFlash2
drafts (MTP stays off under --experts remote).

- AfdEngine(parallel=); cuda_engine passes --parallel on the --experts remote path. Before the wire opens it refuses
  --parallel past 4 and the two settings that need CUDA graphs across the wire: TF_GLM_MULTI_DEPTH=joint (it times
  the batched graphs) and TF_GLM_MULTI_LONE=1 (the one-stream graphs).
- The wire's per-forward hook (afd.begin) at the two forwards #243 adds: BatchedVerify.forward and
  decode.prefill_chunk (chunk=True, like prefill's chunks). MultiDecoder captures no batched windows when the weights
  carry the AFD wire, and BatchedVerify.capture refuses there. TF_GLM_MULTI_GRAPHS is ignored under the split (the
  startup line says so).
- World 1: MultiDecoder applies its ops as it decides them, and seals, numbers and sends no per-iteration message
  (there is no follower rank); _time_rows gathers by the comm's world. The --parallel estimate's extra stream slots,
  the multi-stream drafter's rings and the kept states take the engine's world (a world-1 slot is twice a TP2 rank's).
- Kept prompts: at world 1 the engine keeps at most what the startup estimate leaves room for (kept_most(entries,
  spare, state), at least one); the pool sizing reserves the same count.
- #301's vote needs no glue: the attention node samples alone over afd.Solo's gather, and the expert nodes never
  sample; they wait for the next forward's header.
- Unchanged: the expert nodes, the wire and its settings. Batched windows of 9 to 32 rows take the experts' 2,048-row
  prompt scratch.

Single-stream serving (--parallel 1, the default) is unchanged: every edit is behind parallel > 1 or #243's own paths.

Tests: tests/test_glm_multi_afd.py (CPU: world 1 through the real MultiDecoder, BatchedVerify.forward and
prefill_chunk on tests/test_glm_multi.py's fake model; every reply equals its solo draft:false reply),
tests/test_glm_serial_stop_afd.py (CPU: #301's loops over afd.Solo), tests/cuda/test_glm_segments_world1.py and
tests/cuda/test_glm_expert_rows_window.py (GPU: the segmented kernels at world-1 shapes; an expert row's bits alone
and inside batched windows).

Credits: GLM-5.3-Flash multi-stream decode, sealed rank messages and the stop vote by Mia's AI Lab (@MiaAI-Lab),
ashhart/TensorFold#243 and #301, from their GLM-5.3-Flash EXL3 2x DGX Sparks recipe (Apache-2.0). Aditya
Thyagarajan (@aditya1503) wrote ashhart/TensorFold#200, MTP concurrency, which #243 superseded. TensorFold
(Apache-2.0 since v0.6.0) by Ash Hart (@ashhart).

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (planning) and GLM-5.3-Flash (execution), via Hermes

